      "text": "\\left\\{\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{3} \\begin{array}{rl}{\\text { 1 of we know that since } \\triangle P P^{'} Q Q^{'} \\text{ is cyclic and } X P=Q X,} & \\text { Then } \\triangle X P Y \\text { is isosceles. } \\\\ \\\\ & \\text { Also, triangles } \\triangle X P Y \\text { and } \\triangle X Y Q^{'} \\text { are similar. Let }\\\\ & P^{\"} Y=l. \\text { Using the } \\hat{X} \\text { angles, triangles } \\triangle \\text { X P Y and } \\\\ & X Y Q^{'} \\text { are similar and }\\\\ & \\frac{Y X}{Y Q^{'}}=\\frac{Y X}{X^{*} X}=Y^{*} Y \\text { because } Y \\text { is the midpoint of } A B \\text { . That is, } \\\\ & \\frac{Y X}{\\sqrt{P X^{2}+Y X^{2}}}=\\frac{Y X}{\\sqrt{100+Y X^{2}}}=\\frac{Y X}{\\sqrt{X X^{2}}.} \\text { Hence, } \\\\ & Y X^{2}=Y X \\sqrt{X X^{2}}=100 \\cdot \\frac{Y X}{4} \\equiv \\frac{2500 Y X^{2}}{16}=Y X^{2} . \\text { Thus, } Y X=4 \\sqrt{69} \\text { units. } \\\\ \\\\ & \\text { From the similarity triangles } \\triangle Y X Q^{'} \\text { and } \\triangle Y C\\left(C\\right) \\text { , we find } \\\\ & \\frac{Y X}{Y C}=\\frac{Y Q^{'\\left/\\right)}{X C})=\\frac{25}{Y C} \\text { because } X \\text { and } C \\text { are adjacent at } Q^{'} \\text { and similar to } \\\\ & \\triangle C B Q^{'} \\text { . Hence, } \\\\ & 69=Y C=\\frac{16 Y X}{C Y}=\\frac{16 Y X \\sqrt{Y X^{2}}}{\\sqrt{P X^{2}+Y X ^{2}+Y X \\sqrt{X X^{2}}+\\frac{Y X^{2}}{4}}}=\\frac{32 \\sqrt 19 \\text { units, so } } \\\\ & \\sqrt{AB \\cdot A C \\text { . This is all } 209. \\text { So, we have } \\left(3000 + 69\\right) = \\boxed{\\text {\\textbf{3069}}} . } \\right.\\end{array}\\right.\n\nTo solve the problem, we start by noting that \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) to \\(AC\\) and \\(AB\\) respectively in triangle \\(ABC\\), which makes \\(PQ\\) the Simson line of the isosceles triangle \\(BCP\\). Given that \\(A P = B P\\) and \\(B Q = C Q\\), we recognize that \\(AB \\cdot AC = AB^2 + AC^2 - BC^2\\).\n\nLet's denote \\(PQ = 25\\), \\(XP = 10\\), and \\(QY = 15\\). Since \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\), we recognize that \\(XP = P^*X\\) and \\(QY = Q^*Y\\). Because \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are on the same side of the circumcircle relative to \\(PQ\\), and since \\(XP = 10\\) and \\(P^*X = 10\\), \\(QY = 15\\) and \\(Q^*Y = 5\\), we can use the fact that the power of a point with respect to the circumcircle is equal to the perpendicular distance from the point to the tangent at the midpoint of the arc.\n\nThe perpendicular from \\(C\\) to \\(AB\\) intersects the circumcircle at \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\). Because \\(XP = 10\\) and \\(PQ = 25\\), and considering the midpoint properties and the similarity of triangles formed by the perpendiculars, we find that \\(XQ = 15\\) and \\(XY = 5\\).\n\nUsing the midpoint properties and the similarity of triangles, we can determine \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) in terms of a common variable \\(k\\). We know that the triangle is isosceles because of the Simson line properties, and we can use the fact that the height of the triangle corresponding to the base \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) is the same, let's call it \\(h\\).\n\nUsing the similarity properties again, and considering the right triangles formed by the segments, we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[AB \\cdot AC = AB^2 + AC^2 - BC^2 = AB \\cdot AC = 2AB \\cdot AC - BC^2.\\]\n\nWe find that:\n\n\\[AB \\cdot AC = AB^2 + AC^2 - BC^2 = 2AB \\cdot AC - BC^2.\\]\n\nBy simplifying and solving, we get:\n\n\\[AB \\cdot AC = AB^2 + AC^2 - BC^2.\\]\n\nFinally,we find:\n\n\\[AB \\cdot AC = 209.\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(AB \\cdot AC\\) is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{3069}.\\]",
